Subject: [PATCH 31/31] property.fs: split SIZE and WRITE, so the caller can name the destination
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 16, the first deliverable of the storage decision, which is
REVIEW-preboot-forth-binary-structures.md's F2 -- the only structural
blocker between this lab and the poke-like toolkit it exists to reach.

F2: there is no "encode INTO this address" primitive anywhere in the
1275 wordset. Every encode-* word chooses its own destination via
alloc-tree, so it cannot be aimed at a flash region, an MMIO window, a
CBFS entry or a boot-handoff page. poke maps a type over storage that
already exists; encode-* constructs fresh bytes in an arena you do not
pick. The read half generalizes; the write half does not exist.

NOT A PLUGGABLE ALLOCATOR, and the reason is in TODO 16. alloc-tree is
called INSIDE encode-int, where the caller cannot reach it, so a
"destination" it consulted would have to live in a variable -- a mode
flag wearing a parameter's clothes, and a cached fact about which arena
you are in is the bug class this lab keeps finding. It would also leave
the device-tree path and the toolkit path sharing one mutable global.

Instead: each encode word was already

    size -> alloc-tree -> write at the address alloc-tree chose

with the write step ALREADY parameterised by an address (l!-be, move).
So the sizer and the writer are exposed and the 1275 word is redefined in
terms of them:

    /int  /string  /bytes      sizers
    int!  string!  bytes!      writers -- destination is a stack parameter
    encode-int ...             unchanged signature: sizer, alloc-tree, writer

Same signatures. ONE implementation per encoding, used by the device tree
and by anything aimed elsewhere -- extract the shipped thing, never
re-implement it, which the pluggable version could not satisfy. The two
paths share no mutable state at all: not a global, not a mode.

string! writes the terminator itself. alloc-tree zero-fills and the old
encode-string inherited that; a caller's buffer is not pre-zeroed.

int! inherits 13.2(b)'s refusal for free, because it IS l!-be: a value
that cannot survive four bytes is refused wherever it is aimed.

THE CHECKPOINT IS `here` UNCHANGED, and that is the whole of it. Bytes
landing at the right address is satisfied by a writer that allocates in
the arena and copies; only a writer that leaves HERE alone can ever be
pointed at memory the firmware does not own. property-abi asserts both,
because neither is sufficient alone -- writing nothing moves HERE just as
little.

WATCHED TO BITE, and the first two attempts were BAD CONTROLS.

Making int! allocate-and-write-elsewhere, or do nothing at all, broke
every property in the device tree and the probe never ran: the generic
"probe did not complete" gate fired instead of the named one. That is
CLAUDE.md's own rule -- scope the fault to the subject under test -- and
I broke it. A fault that breaks everything sends every scenario to the
same rung.

The narrowed pair isolates one assertion each, with the other still
passing, which is what proves they are jointly necessary:

    int! also bumps here          -> s-int-HERE-MOVED, BYTES-MATCH still ok
    int! writes one byte late,
      only for the probe's value  -> s-int-HERE-UNCHANGED, BYTES-DIFFER

The second is scoped to a value the device tree never encodes, so the
firmware stays up and only the subject fails. string! losing its
terminator fails by name too, against a buffer poisoned with ff first so
that an inherited zero cannot pass for a written one.

diff --git a/forth/device/property.fs b/forth/device/property.fs
--- a/forth/device/property.fs
+++ b/forth/device/property.fs
@@ -316,21 +316,71 @@
 \ 5.3.5.1 Property array encoding
 \ 
 
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 16: SIZE and WRITE, split apart.
+\
+\ Every encode word below was `size -> alloc-tree -> write at the address
+\ alloc-tree chose`, with the write step already parameterised by an address
+\ (`l!-be`, `move`). REVIEW-preboot-forth-binary-structures.md's F2 is that the
+\ chooser is not the caller: there is no "encode INTO this address" primitive
+\ anywhere in the wordset, so it cannot be aimed at a flash region, an MMIO
+\ window, a CBFS entry or a boot-handoff page. poke maps a type over storage
+\ that already exists; this constructs fresh bytes in an arena you do not pick.
+\
+\ THE FIX IS NOT A PLUGGABLE ALLOCATOR. `alloc-tree` is called INSIDE
+\ encode-int, where the caller cannot reach it, so a "destination" it consulted
+\ would have to live in a variable -- a mode flag wearing a parameter's clothes,
+\ and a cached fact about which arena you are in is the bug class this lab keeps
+\ finding. It would also leave the device-tree path and the toolkit path sharing
+\ one mutable global.
+\
+\ So the sizer and the writer are exposed, and the 1275 word is redefined in
+\ terms of them. Same signatures, ONE implementation per encoding used by both
+\ paths, destination a genuine stack parameter, and no shared mutable state --
+\ not a global, not a mode.
+\
+\ THE ASSERTION THAT MATTERS IS `here` UNCHANGED across a writer call. That is
+\ what separates writing where you were told from writing into the arena and
+\ copying, and only the first can ever be aimed at memory the firmware does not
+\ own. property-abi checks exactly that.
+\
+\ `int!` inherits 13.2(b)'s refusal for free, because it IS `l!-be`: a value
+\ that cannot survive four bytes is refused wherever it is aimed.
+
+: /int    ( -- n )                          /l ;
+: /string ( str len -- n )                  nip char+ ;
+: /bytes  ( data-addr data-len -- n )       nip ;
+
+: int!    ( n dest -- )                     l!-be ;
+
+: string! ( str len dest -- )
+  \ The caller's buffer is not pre-zeroed the way alloc-tree's is, so the
+  \ terminator is written here rather than inherited.
+  2dup + >r        ( str len dest )   ( R: dest+len )
+  swap move
+  0 r> c!
+  ;
+
+: bytes!  ( data-addr data-len dest -- )    swap move ;
+
 : encode-int    ( n -- prop-addr prop-len )
-  /l alloc-tree tuck l!-be /l
+  /int alloc-tree tuck int! /int
   ;
 
 : encode-string ( str len -- prop-addr prop-len )
   \ we trust len here. should probably check string?
-  tuck char+ alloc-tree ( len str prop-addr )
-  tuck 3 pick move      ( len prop-addr )
-  swap 1+
+  2dup /string           ( str len n )
+  dup alloc-tree         ( str len n addr )
+  swap >r  dup >r        ( str len addr )   ( R: n addr )
+  string!
+  r> r>                  ( addr n )
   ;
 
 : encode-bytes ( data-addr data-len -- prop-addr prop-len )
-  tuck alloc-tree ( len str prop-addr )
-  tuck 3 pick move
-  swap
+  2dup /bytes            ( a l n )
+  dup alloc-tree         ( a l n addr )
+  swap >r  dup >r        ( a l addr )       ( R: n addr )
+  bytes!
+  r> r>                  ( addr n )
   ;
 
 \ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.2(c).
